From 565bfb63a3849a7f10bc453aeaed3e59e3f984de Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Tue, 4 Aug 2026 21:04:02 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Build item 3-1's shared half: a guarded numeric tree

The census in 0083 said the generic arithmetic operators are handed two Numbers on
73 817 515 of 73 818 646 invocations -- 86.6% of every box the corpus allocates -- while
the compiler's own proof reaches 0.75% of them. The operator already receives two Numbers
whatever they are stored in; what it cannot do is hand one back, because its consumer is a
JSValue. So the shared half is not a typed backing store. It is a run-time-guarded
specialization of an arithmetic expression TREE: evaluate each leaf once into a temporary,
test the leaves for Number, compute the whole tree on raw doubles, box only the root.

Evaluation order is the whole correctness argument and is why the rule is narrower than
the census. The ordinary emission evaluates a node's operands and THEN coerces them, so in
a nested tree a coercion runs between two leaf evaluations -- and a coercion is observable,
because ToPrimitive on an object runs valueOf. A tree is therefore eligible only when every
leaf evaluated after the first internal node in postorder can neither cause nor observe
anything: a numeric literal or a proven-numeric local. Leaves before the first coercion are
unrestricted, which is not a corner -- precedence makes `s + a[0] * 1.5` right-leaning, so
all three of its leaves qualify. `(a[0] * 2) + p.v` does not, and is refused.

When the guard holds nothing is skipped: every operator here applies ToNumeric (for `+`,
ToPrimitive then ToNumeric) to both operands, and on a Number each is the identity. The
native forms are TryCreateNativeNumericValue's, the same ones the all-native path emits, so
the arms are identical by construction rather than by inspection.

Measured on the corpus, one build, the switch the only difference:

  suite         generic off -> on   ratio      boxes off -> on   ratio  trees
  Richards        55 198   49 204   0.891     13 659    10 743   0.787     12
  DeltaBlue       14 533   13 933   0.959      6 765     6 765   1.000      6
  RayTrace       844 051  796 759   0.944    841 017   823 293   0.979     27
  Box2D       15 916 294 13 870 770 0.871 11 434 706 10 663 471  0.933    424
  EarleyBoyer     26 116       79   0.003    563 997   563 997   1.000    129
  Crypto      39 872 917 23 249 552 0.583 42 412 174 33 356 341  0.786    191
  NavierStokes17 094 558 15 373 914 0.899 29 977 465 29 423 391  0.982     73
  TOTAL       73 823 667 53 354 211 0.723 85 249 783 74 848 001  0.878    862

10 401 782 boxes removed -- 12.2% of everything the corpus allocates, from 862 compiled
sites. Items 3-0, 3-3, 3-5, 3-7 and 3-1's bitwise half moved 0.36% between them.

It is well short of the 86.6% ceiling, and the per-suite column explains rather than
excuses that. Crypto is what it was built for. NavierStokes loses 10.1% of its generic
invocations and 1.8% of its boxes, and EarleyBoyer loses 99.7% of its invocations and not
one box -- so the great majority of those two suites' boxes are minted somewhere that is
not a binary arithmetic operator. The census bounded what the operators could reach; it did
not say the boxes were all at the operators, and that is the next count.

One mistake, caught by measuring. The first eligibility rule required two operators, on the
argument that a single node mints one box either way -- true of the result and wrong,
because it forgets the operand: `a[0] * 2` costs two boxes today, the literal and the
result, since only `+` has a JSValue x double overload. That rule took the corpus from
10.4 M boxes removed to 5.6 M, Crypto alone losing 4.7 M. What ships counts what the guard
buys: (operators - 1) + native leaves >= 1.

Not yet measured: wall clock. These are exact counters and no interleaved timing run has
been taken.

NumericSpeculationTests: 33 cases, each on both settings of BROILER_JS_NUMERIC_SPECULATION
-- values (NaN, both infinities, -0, ToInt32 wrapping and shift masking), types (string
concatenation, valueOf, BigInt mixing, null/undefined/booleans) and ORDER (a getter read
once and left to right, a valueOf that mutates a later leaf, a getter that mutates a later
leaf, a throwing leaf that must stop the next being read). Plus two counter assertions,
because all 33 also pass when the specialization never fires. 0083's census fixture is
updated to measure with speculation off -- it counts what reaches the generic path, and
this is what takes shapes off it -- and now asserts that the same shape with speculation on
reaches it zero times.

Full repository suite 7 962 tests, 0 failures.

